  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nI propose to use a heuristic optimization approach leveraging a population of Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) particles to explore the sequence space. The key idea is to initialize several structured candidates across different lengths and then iteratively refine them using PSO. The rationale is twofold: first, different sequence lengths may yield better objectives overall, while second, structured initializations (instead of random) provide more promising starting points than and escape local optima faster than traditional random restarts. The approach directly targets the objective function's structure by forcing the self-convolution to be bounded while maximizing the sum.\n\nNotable limitations include:\n- The evaluated candidates may not be mathematically optimal but likely fall below the target upper bound 1.5027.\n- Convergence depends on balancing exploration and theiriation steps, which requires careful parameterization.\n- The method may get trapped by the sequence length decision affecting objective scaling.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification and Solving Strategy\nThe evaluator defines the objective as:\nf = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a, mode='full')) / (sum(a) ** 2)\n\nPSO formulation:\n- Particle vector: a_{i} \u2208 \u211d\u207f with a_{i} \u2265 0, sum(a_{i}) \u2265 0.01\n- Objective function: A string-based controller to minimize f\n- Fitness constraint: Valid evaluations return a finite f, else we view as terminated (inf)\n- Particle movement: \n    F_i(k) = (1 - alpha) * F_{prev,i} + (previous best for the particle)\n    lambda = gausian noise (beta) from adjacent convolution indices\n    Compound structure = (global_best_trend) * uniform_vector + (local_best) * discontinuous_vector + (alpha_period) * cyclic_vector\n\nThe algorithm will:\n- Initialize each particle structure in one of four ways: clustered with decay, periodic, cosine wrapped or uniform\n- Use inclusive elitism: retain the best global and local solution\n- Termination criteria: 1500 seconds or stable_iters exceeding (both global and local)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nSteps:\n1. Import and structure random/pseudo-random sequences with modes and length control.\n2. Initialize candidate with multiple structured sequences across defined length ranges\n3. For each sequence length, generate: \n   - Uniform sequence (all ones)\n   - Clustered sequence (decaying from center)\n   - Periodic sequence (clamped sinusoidal repetition)\n   - Cosine-based sequence (wrapped and smoothed)\n4. For each candidate:\n   - Scale to ensure sum >= 0.01\n   - Compute exact objective using numerical evaluation\n5. Apply PSO movement rules:\n   - Track particle positions for history\n   - Update velocities using global and local best\n   - Apply gaussian random perturbation focusing on convolution peak regions\n6. Introduce a \"candidate block\" that defines the sequence structure:\n   - Uniform block\n   - Clustered (center-weighted)\n   - Periodic\n   - Cosine basis structure\n7. Ensure particles remain within [0, 1000] range\n   sum(spec) >= 0.01\n   convolution mode = 'full'\n8. Iterate until timeout or convergence, or manually break after achieving target.\n\n4. Search Hyperparameters and Variations\nDefault hyperparameters:\n- Length ranges: [100, 500, 600, 1000]\n- Particle count: 20\n- Main iterations: 500\n- Move chance: 0.8\n- Noise amount: 0.3\n- Swarm size: 3 (per length)\n- Max unraveling factor: 0.05\n- Random step noise: 0.15\n\nThe implementation will:\n- Search for improvements at most within each length group\n- Use iterative refinement at each state\n- Check timeouts regularly\n\n5. Execution Budget and Termination\nApproximate time to complete PSO iteration for n=1000:\n- 1000^2 per candidate * 500 candidates \u2248 500 million operations\n- Python numpy convolve: ~1e6-2e6 per second\n- Estimated time for n=1000 iteration: 200-300 seconds (for the convolution part)\n- Total time for all lengths, scaling and iterations: similar to two full runs, but with progress monitoring\n\nThe plan will:\n- Monitor active time and break refinements when more than ~95% time consumed\n- Retain the best candidate from all methods \n- If timeout hits, return the best candidate so far\n\n6. Validation and Return Structure\nThe final candidate will be:\n- Evaluated with the exact function\n- Rounded to 5 decimals to match the evaluator's precision requirements\n- Returned as a floating-point vector passed to the final evaluate_sequence\n\nThe algorithm ensures:\n- All candidates meet sum \u2265 0.01\n- No negative values\n- Within [0, 1000] bounds\n- Simple PSO-inspired i.iterations\n\nThis approach balances potential for near-optimality with computation budget, systematically construct different structure and length candidates and validate each against the principal metric.",
